[[eco-freezing]]
=== Freezing

[abstract]
An environment changes as programs are installed and packages solved.
*Freezing* captures it as it stands in a container image that runs the same
programs and serves the same views on another machine, with nothing
mounted from the machine that built it. This section specifies what a
frozen image contains, what it requires of its host, how it decides who may
reach it, and what a slim image gives up.

**Which environments.** Only a Docker or Podman environment provisioned
from a release can be frozen. A native or Apptainer environment has no
image to build on, and a development environment's compiler lives outside
it. The image is built by the environment's own engine, on the machine that
holds the environment, and freezing changes nothing in the environment.

**Contents.** The image is the environment's image with:

 - the toolchain installed from the environment's lock, without solving
   again, so its packages are exactly those the environment was tested
   with; a lock that does not install fails the freeze;
 - the Morloc home (<<eco-overview>>);
 - every installed program, with its build directory and its copied source
   directory, and the installed module source;
 - the views and eval allow-list, as the image's default command.

An environment that is missing any of these, for instance because it was
never fully built, is refused with the missing part named.

**What is refused.** Each program travels as the directory `mim install`
copied, so freezing inspects them first. Tool state that nothing reads at
run time -- version-control directories, caches, virtual environments,
build output -- is refused. A program over 100 MB, a file over 50 MB, or an
environment with no programs at all must be confirmed by the user; without
a terminal to ask, confirmation is refusal. A force option answers all of
these.

**Running the image.** The default command starts the front end
(<<eco-serving>>) listening on every interface on port 8080. Inside a
container that is the only way a published port can reach it, so the image
does not decide who can reach it: whoever runs it does, with the port
mapping and the network. A token, if given at run time in
`MORLOC_MCP_TOKEN`, is required exactly as on any server. Eval stays locked
without a token unless `MORLOC_EVAL_ALLOW_NO_AUTH` is set. An environment
with no views gives an image with no default command; its programs run when
named.

Morloc moves data between pools through shared memory, which container
engines limit by default; the image records the size the environment used
in a label so that the operator can grant it.

**Labels.** Once it leaves the manager nothing tracks the image, so it
describes itself: the Morloc version, the source environment, the programs,
each adapter's modules, whether eval is on, and the suggested shared-memory
size.

**Slim images.** A slim image omits what only building needs: compilers,
the solver, the Morloc compiler, the runtime's headers and source, and the
build-only packages, found by walking the solved packages' dependencies from
the requested ones without passing through a build tool. It keeps every
interpreter and library the programs load. Since eval compiles code, an
environment with eval enabled cannot be frozen slim. After a slim image is
built, every program is started inside it and every runtime library's
links are resolved, so a missing library is found at freeze time rather
than in production.

